{"module":"place","voice":"female","duration":185.2,"segments":[{"el":"title","text":"Place: who gets to live where","start":0.0,"end":2.07,"words":[[0.0,0.48],[0.75,0.91],[0.91,1.23],[1.23,1.39],[1.39,1.71],[1.71,1.95]]},{"el":"intro","text":"Most American neighborhoods didn't become segregated by accident or by choice alone.","start":2.87,"end":7.97,"words":[[2.92,3.25],[3.25,3.79],[3.79,4.39],[4.39,4.72],[4.72,5.06],[5.06,5.79],[5.79,5.99],[5.99,6.53],[6.53,6.59],[6.59,6.8],[6.8,7.13],[7.13,7.53]]},{"el":"intro","text":"Governments drew the lines, and many of those lines are still visible on the map.","start":8.32,"end":13.4,"words":[[8.36,9.1],[9.1,9.32],[9.32,9.46],[9.46,9.9],[10.16,10.38],[10.38,10.67],[10.67,10.82],[10.82,11.11],[11.11,11.48],[11.48,11.55],[11.55,11.85],[11.85,12.36],[12.36,12.51],[12.51,12.65],[12.65,12.95]]},{"el":"h0","text":"Racial zoning, then covenants","start":13.75,"end":16.09,"words":[[13.8,14.25],[14.25,14.79],[15.05,15.28],[15.28,15.96]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Baltimore passed the first citywide racial zoning law in 1910; the Supreme Court struck racial zoning down in Buchanan v. Warley (1917).","start":16.79,"end":27.41,"words":[[16.87,17.43],[17.43,17.75],[17.75,17.91],[17.91,18.23],[18.23,18.87],[18.87,19.35],[19.35,19.83],[19.83,19.99],[19.99,20.15],[20.15,21.02],[21.3,21.46],[21.46,21.94],[21.94,22.18],[22.18,22.58],[22.58,23.06],[23.06,23.54],[23.54,23.86],[23.86,24.02],[24.02,24.66],[24.66,24.9],[25.29,25.61],[25.61,26.89]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Developers and neighbors turned to racial covenants written into deeds, which the Court let stand in 1926.","start":27.76,"end":35.19,"words":[[27.81,28.47],[28.47,28.69],[28.69,29.13],[29.13,29.42],[29.42,29.57],[29.57,30.01],[30.01,30.67],[30.67,31.03],[31.03,31.33],[31.33,31.69],[31.95,32.24],[32.24,32.39],[32.39,32.61],[32.61,32.83],[32.83,33.2],[33.2,33.34],[33.34,34.66]]},{"el":"s0","text":"In Shelley v. Kraemer (1948) it ruled courts couldn't enforce them, but the language remains in millions of deeds.","start":35.54,"end":43.99,"words":[[35.59,35.74],[35.74,36.05],[36.05,36.28],[36.67,37.05],[37.05,38.21],[38.48,38.64],[38.64,38.94],[38.94,39.25],[39.25,39.72],[39.72,40.1],[40.1,40.41],[40.68,40.91],[40.91,41.07],[41.07,41.68],[41.68,42.23],[42.23,42.38],[42.38,42.92],[42.92,43.07],[43.07,43.46]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Some states, such as Minnesota (2019), now let owners formally strike it.","start":44.34,"end":50.49,"words":[[44.34,44.57],[44.57,45.12],[45.39,45.7],[45.7,45.86],[45.86,46.56],[46.56,47.65],[47.92,48.15],[48.15,48.39],[48.39,48.78],[48.78,49.24],[49.24,49.71],[49.71,49.94]]},{"el":"s0","text":"The map's covenants layer shows more than 100,000 of them.","start":50.84,"end":55.07,"words":[[50.89,51.04],[51.04,51.32],[51.32,51.98],[51.98,52.27],[52.27,52.55],[52.55,52.7],[52.7,52.92],[52.92,54.15],[54.15,54.29],[54.29,54.58]]},{"el":"h1","text":"Redlining","start":55.97,"end":56.9,"words":[[56.02,56.85]]},{"el":"s1","text":"From 1935 to 1940 the federal Home Owners' Loan Corporation graded neighborhoods in about 200 cities; Black and immigrant neighborhoods were colored red as “hazardous.”","start":57.6,"end":69.37,"words":[[57.6,57.9],[57.9,59.03],[59.03,59.18],[59.18,60.01],[60.01,60.16],[60.16,60.69],[60.69,60.99],[60.99,61.37],[61.37,61.67],[61.67,62.42],[62.42,62.95],[62.95,63.63],[63.63,63.78],[63.78,64.16],[64.16,64.84],[64.84,65.29],[65.55,65.86],[65.86,66.08],[66.08,66.68],[66.68,67.36],[67.36,67.51],[67.51,67.89],[67.89,68.12],[68.12,68.27],[68.27,68.87]]},{"el":"s1","text":"The Federal Housing Administration refused to insure loans in or near Black neighborhoods and recommended covenants.","start":69.72,"end":76.29,"words":[[69.77,69.9],[69.9,70.37],[70.37,70.77],[70.77,71.7],[71.7,72.17],[72.17,72.3],[72.3,72.64],[72.64,72.97],[72.97,73.1],[73.1,73.17],[73.17,73.37],[73.37,73.64],[73.64,74.24],[74.24,74.44],[74.44,75.1],[75.1,75.77]]},{"el":"s1","text":"In Detroit in 1941, a developer built a half-mile wall to separate a new white subdivision from a Black neighborhood so the FHA would back its loans.","start":76.64,"end":86.81,"words":[[76.69,76.84],[76.84,77.36],[77.36,77.51],[77.51,78.63],[78.89,79.04],[79.04,79.64],[79.64,79.93],[79.93,80.08],[80.08,80.61],[80.61,80.83],[80.83,80.98],[80.98,81.43],[81.43,81.57],[81.57,81.8],[81.8,82.1],[82.1,82.84],[82.84,83.14],[83.14,83.29],[83.29,83.59],[83.59,84.19],[84.19,84.41],[84.41,84.56],[84.56,85.16],[85.16,85.38],[85.38,85.6],[85.6,85.83],[85.83,86.27]]},{"el":"s1","text":"Studies find formerly redlined areas today are poorer, have lower life expectancy, and run several degrees hotter in summer.","start":87.16,"end":95.73,"words":[[87.21,87.68],[87.68,88.07],[88.07,88.55],[88.55,89.17],[89.17,89.65],[89.65,90.04],[90.04,90.12],[90.12,90.59],[90.87,91.1],[91.1,91.42],[91.42,91.73],[91.73,92.67],[92.95,93.18],[93.18,93.42],[93.42,93.89],[93.89,94.36],[94.36,94.68],[94.68,94.83],[94.83,95.23]]},{"el":"h2","text":"Sundown towns and white flight","start":96.63,"end":98.97,"words":[[96.68,97.31],[97.31,97.76],[97.76,98.02],[98.02,98.38],[98.38,98.83]]},{"el":"s2","text":"Historian James Loewen documented hundreds of towns that kept Black residents out after dark, by sign, ordinance or violence (see the old laws tracker).","start":99.67,"end":110.15,"words":[[99.71,100.42],[100.42,100.9],[100.9,101.37],[101.37,102.24],[102.24,102.87],[102.87,103.03],[103.03,103.43],[103.43,103.66],[103.66,103.98],[103.98,104.29],[104.29,105.0],[105.0,105.24],[105.24,105.56],[105.56,105.87],[106.15,106.39],[106.39,106.78],[107.06,107.61],[107.61,107.69],[107.69,108.32],[108.32,108.48],[108.48,108.64],[108.64,108.95],[108.95,109.19],[109.19,109.66]]},{"el":"s2","text":"As Black families moved north and into new neighborhoods, real estate agents practiced “blockbusting,” scaring white owners into selling cheaply and reselling to Black families at a markup.","start":110.5,"end":121.72,"words":[[110.55,110.69],[110.69,110.99],[110.99,111.51],[111.51,111.81],[111.81,112.03],[112.03,112.25],[112.25,112.55],[112.55,112.77],[112.77,113.51],[113.77,114.07],[114.07,114.52],[114.52,115.11],[115.11,115.7],[115.7,116.52],[116.52,117.04],[117.04,117.34],[117.34,117.71],[117.71,118.0],[118.0,118.37],[118.37,118.82],[118.82,119.04],[119.04,119.56],[119.56,119.71],[119.71,120.01],[120.01,120.53],[120.53,120.68],[120.68,120.82],[120.82,121.27]]},{"el":"s2","text":"The Fair Housing Act passed on April 11, 1968, a week after Martin Luther King Jr. was killed.","start":122.07,"end":129.67,"words":[[122.11,122.26],[122.26,122.47],[122.47,122.9],[122.9,123.12],[123.12,123.41],[123.41,123.55],[123.55,123.98],[123.98,124.48],[124.74,126.03],[126.28,126.42],[126.42,126.64],[126.64,126.93],[126.93,127.29],[127.29,127.57],[127.57,127.79],[127.79,128.22],[128.58,128.8],[128.8,129.16]]},{"el":"h3","text":"Does zoning still keep neighborhoods apart?","start":130.57,"end":132.92,"words":[[130.63,130.81],[130.81,131.18],[131.18,131.43],[131.43,131.61],[131.61,132.17],[132.17,132.48]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Explicit racial rules are illegal, but zoning that allows only single-family homes on large lots raises the price of entry to many neighborhoods.","start":133.62,"end":142.46,"words":[[133.67,134.32],[134.32,134.75],[134.75,135.03],[135.03,135.1],[135.1,135.6],[135.85,136.07],[136.07,136.5],[136.5,136.71],[136.71,137.07],[137.07,137.43],[137.43,138.29],[138.29,138.64],[138.64,138.79],[138.79,139.07],[139.07,139.36],[139.36,139.79],[139.79,139.93],[139.93,140.29],[140.29,140.43],[140.43,140.79],[140.79,140.93],[140.93,141.22],[141.22,141.93]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Researchers broadly agree such rules preserve economic segregation; they debate how much they preserve racial segregation today, compared with income differences, preferences and discrimination by landlords and lenders, which fair-housing tests still find.","start":142.81,"end":161.0,"words":[[142.86,143.51],[143.51,144.0],[144.0,144.33],[144.33,144.65],[144.65,144.98],[144.98,145.47],[145.47,146.12],[146.12,147.1],[147.39,147.63],[147.63,148.12],[148.12,148.37],[148.37,148.69],[148.69,148.94],[148.94,149.43],[149.43,149.92],[149.92,150.82],[150.82,151.31],[151.59,152.16],[152.16,152.41],[152.41,152.82],[152.82,153.63],[153.92,154.73],[154.73,154.98],[154.98,156.12],[156.12,156.37],[156.37,157.02],[157.02,157.27],[157.27,157.84],[158.12,158.45],[158.45,159.18],[159.18,159.59],[159.59,159.92],[159.92,160.41]]},{"el":"h4","text":"What to do","start":161.9,"end":162.95,"words":[[161.95,162.29],[162.29,162.52],[162.52,162.75]]},{"el":"s4","text":"Look up your neighborhood on the redlining and covenant layers.","start":163.65,"end":166.93,"words":[[163.69,163.88],[163.88,164.0],[164.0,164.12],[164.12,164.61],[164.61,164.73],[164.73,164.85],[164.85,165.4],[165.4,165.58],[165.58,166.07],[166.07,166.43]]},{"el":"s4","text":"If your deed has a covenant and your state allows it, have it struck.","start":167.28,"end":171.37,"words":[[167.33,167.47],[167.47,167.61],[167.61,167.81],[167.81,168.02],[168.02,168.16],[168.16,168.7],[168.7,168.91],[168.91,169.05],[169.05,169.39],[169.39,169.74],[169.74,169.94],[170.18,170.39],[170.39,170.53],[170.53,170.94]]},{"el":"s4","text":"Support fair-housing testing, inclusive zoning reform and down-payment help for first-time buyers in formerly redlined areas.","start":171.72,"end":179.58,"words":[[171.77,172.13],[172.13,172.79],[172.79,173.31],[173.56,174.15],[174.15,174.59],[174.59,174.96],[174.96,175.18],[175.18,175.98],[175.98,176.28],[176.28,176.42],[176.42,177.01],[177.01,177.38],[177.38,177.52],[177.52,177.96],[177.96,178.55],[178.55,179.06]]},{"el":"s4","text":"(See also the modules on wealth and on communities destroyed.)","start":179.93,"end":184.28,"words":[[179.93,180.09],[180.09,180.51],[180.51,180.68],[180.68,181.27],[181.27,181.43],[181.43,181.77],[181.77,182.02],[182.02,182.19],[182.19,183.02],[183.02,183.78]]}]}